Author:     welpy-cw <h.w.forms@arcor.de>
AuthorDate: Thu Jul 2 09:14:12 2026 -0700
Commit:     daniel@thunderbird.net <daniel@thunderbird.net>
CommitDate: Thu Jul 2 21:44:19 2026 +0000

    Bug 1243658 - Implement cascading tri-state logic for collapsed checkbox tree rows. r=darktrojan,freaktechnik

    Previously, collapsed parent rows showed only their own literal checked
    state, hiding whether any descendants were checked. This patch adds full
    tri-state checkbox semantics where collapsed parents act as a branch
    overview — displaying checked, indeterminate, or unchecked based on the
    aggregate state of the entire subtree.

    Design decision: a collapsed parent is shown as fully checked only when
    *both* the parent and all its descendants are checked. Each node's
    "checked" property is treated as an independent explicit choice — a
    parent can be checked while its children are not, and vice versa. In
    that case the collapsed parent displays indeterminate because the
    subtree is in a mixed state.

    Private instance methods added to CheckboxTreeTableRow:

    * #cascadeDown(dataRow, isChecked) — forces all descendants to match the
      parent's new state when a collapsed parent is toggled, keeping every
      intermediate node's descendant cache accurate for upward ripples.

    * #computeChildAggregate(dataRow) — shared helper that computes
      allChecked / someChecked across a row's immediate children. Used by
      both #initializeDescendants (deep, once per node) and #evaluateUpward
      (shallow, on every toggle) to avoid duplicated logic.

    * #initializeDescendants(dataRow) — lazy one-time deep initialization
      that computes and caches the aggregate descendant state as one of
      three mutually exclusive properties (descendants-all-checked,
      descendants-some-checked, descendants-none-checked), whose presence
      also serves as the evaluation flag. Returns "all", "some", or "none"
      for direct use by fillRow().

    * #evaluateUpward(dataRow) — ripples the aggregate computation up the
      ancestor chain after a checkbox toggle. Includes an early-exit
      short-circuit when a parent's aggregate state hasn't changed.

    fillRow() changes:

    * Collapsed group rows now call #initializeDescendants() and use its
      return value ("all", "some", or "none") together with the row's own
      checked state to determine the checkbox's checked / indeterminate /
      unchecked state via a compact two-liner. Expanded rows and leaves
      continue to display only their literal checked state.

    * The previous inline checkbox.onchange closure (re-created on every
      fillRow call) is replaced by a delegated change event listener in
      connectedCallback() that calls the new private #handleCheckboxToggle()
      method. This avoids per-render memory allocation in the virtual DOM.

    #handleCheckboxToggle() cascades the new state to all descendants when
    the toggled row is collapsed, then evaluates upward so ancestors reflect
    the change. A fast repaint of visible rows follows, preserving keyboard
    focus and screen-reader context without a full tree rebuild.

    Added Test:

    * browser_checkboxTree.js — standalone browser test exercising initial
      tri-state display across multiple fixture scenarios (checked and
      unchecked collapsed parents with all, some, or no children checked),
      cascade-down on collapsed parent toggle (all-empty, indeterminate,
      and fully-checked) with data row property verification, ripple-up on
      child toggle with intermediate-step assertions, no-cascade on
      expanded parent toggle, deep 3-level tree cascade-down and ripple-up,
      and keyboard toggle via Space key. Uses a minimal base-tree-view with
      a TreeDataAdapter and checkbox-tree-table-row in a content tab.

    Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D303390

